course: history
lesson: 57
title: China: the Qing and the Opium Wars
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The last imperial dynasty, the Qing, ruled from 1644 to 1912. Mei says the Qing were Chinese like all the others; the teacher says «foreign rulers». The wise answer: the Manchus from the northeast founded it and kept their own customs, but they ruled through Chinese institutions and the empire became one of China's greatest.
explain: Its emperors Kangxi and Qianlong ruled about sixty years each, and in 1793 Qianlong told a British envoy that China already had «all things». Then Britain kept selling opium, a harmful drug, into China; when China destroyed the opium in 1839, Britain went to war, won and took Hong Kong.
explain: Imagine a shop that sells something harmful, and when the town throws it away, the shop's friends arrive with cannons and make the town pay. That is why Chinese history calls 1839 to 1949 the «century of humiliation». Even in Britain at the time, many people, like the young William Gladstone, called the war shameful.
explain: The century was also full of change: a huge civil war, a powerful empress dowager, a lost war with Japan, the Boxer uprising, and finally a two-year-old boy, Puyi, who became the last emperor and ended his life working in a botanical garden.
fact: The Qing dynasty, 1644–1912, was founded by the Manchus from the northeast; it ruled through Chinese institutions, kept Manchu customs and made men wear the queue, a shaved forehead with a long braid.
fact: The Kangxi Emperor ruled 61 years, 1661–1722, the longest reign in Chinese history; his grandson Qianlong ruled about 60 years, 1735–1796. Louis XIV of France ruled even longer, 72 years.
fact: In 1793 Lord Macartney came from Britain asking for more trade and an embassy in Beijing; the Qianlong Emperor answered that China possessed «all things» and had no need of British goods. Historians add that the Qing court was in fact curious about Western things but refused Britain's terms.
fact: In 1839 Commissioner Lin Zexu had more than a thousand tonnes of British opium destroyed; Britain went to war, the First Opium War of 1839–1842, and won.
fact: The Treaty of Nanjing of 1842 forced China to open five ports to foreign trade and to give Hong Kong Island to Britain; many British people at the time opposed the war.
fact: Chinese history calls 1839–1949 the «century of humiliation»: unfair treaties forced on China, parts of Chinese cities run by foreigners and wars with Britain, France, Japan and others; in 1860 British and French troops burned the Old Summer Palace in Beijing.
fact: The Taiping Rebellion, 1850–1864, was led by Hong Xiuquan, who believed he was the younger brother of Jesus; it was one of the deadliest wars in history, and tens of millions died, mostly from famine and disease.
fact: Britain took Hong Kong Island in 1842 and Kowloon in 1860 and rented the New Territories in 1898 for 99 years; Hong Kong returned to China in 1997 under «one country, two systems», which promised it its own laws and way of life.
fact: Empress Dowager Cixi was the most powerful person at the Qing court from 1861 to 1908; navy money was used to rebuild the Summer Palace, where a stone «marble boat» pavilion stands, but how much this weakened the navy is debated.
fact: In the war of 1894–1895 Japan, which had modernised fast, defeated Qing China and took Taiwan.
fact: The Boxers, named after the martial arts they practised, attacked foreigners and Chinese Christians in 1899–1901; some believed their rituals made them bulletproof, and in 1900 the armies of eight foreign countries took Beijing.
fact: Puyi became emperor in 1908 aged two and gave up the throne in 1912; later Japan made him ruler of Manchukuo, a state in the northeast that Japan really controlled, and after prison he worked at Beijing's botanical garden. The 1987 film The Last Emperor tells his life.
exercise: When did the Qing dynasty rule China? | 1644–1912 | 1368–1644 | 960–1279 | 1912–1949
exercise: Who founded the Qing dynasty? | The Manchus from the northeast | The Mongols of Kublai Khan | The British East India Company | The Ming general Zheng He
exercise: What hairstyle did the Qing make men wear? | The queue: shaved forehead and a long braid | A topknot tied up under a tall black silk hat | Hair cut very short all over | Two buns over the ears
exercise: Which emperor had the longest reign in Chinese history? | Kangxi, with 61 years | Qianlong, with 80 years | Puyi, with 4 years | Qin Shi Huang, with 70 years
exercise: Who ruled France for 72 years? | Louis XIV | Napoleon Bonaparte | Charlemagne | King Louis XVI
exercise: What did Britain want from the Macartney mission? | More trade and an embassy in Beijing | To buy a long stretch of the Great Wall | Help against Napoleon | Silkworm eggs to smuggle home to Britain
exercise: What did Commissioner Lin Zexu do with the opium? | Had it destroyed | Sold it to Japan for silver | Gave it back to Britain | Stored it for medicine
exercise: What did China lose in the Treaty of Nanjing? | Hong Kong Island, and five ports were opened | Taiwan and Korea | Beijing, the Forbidden City and all the land around them | Tibet and Mongolia
exercise: What does China call the years 1839 to 1949? | The century of humiliation | The golden century of peace | The century of the Wall | The century of silk
exercise: What happened to the Old Summer Palace in 1860? | British and French troops burned it | It was moved stone by stone to Hong Kong | An earthquake destroyed it | It became a museum
exercise: Who led the Taiping Rebellion? | Hong Xiuquan | Doctor Sun Yat-sen | Commissioner Lin Zexu | Empress Dowager Cixi
exercise: What caused most deaths in the Taiping Rebellion? | Famine and disease | Volcanoes and floods | Only battles at sea | Poison in the rivers
exercise: When did Hong Kong return to China? | 1997 | 1842 | 1949 | 2019
exercise: What does «one country, two systems» mean for Hong Kong? | Part of China, with its own laws and way of life | Two separate countries that share one flag and one army | China and Britain rule it together | Two governments that change each year
exercise: Where is the marble boat? | At the Summer Palace in Beijing | In Hong Kong harbour, next to the ferries | On the Grand Canal | At the Forbidden City gate
exercise: What did Japan take after the 1894–1895 war? | Taiwan | Hong Kong | Beijing | Tibet and Lhasa
exercise: Why were the Boxers called «Boxers»? | They practised martial arts | They fought in boxing rings | They packed tea in boxes | They came from a town called Box
exercise: How old was Puyi when he became emperor? | Two | Twelve | Twenty | Forty
test: Mei says the Qing were just Chinese; the teacher says foreigners. What is wisest? | A Manchu dynasty that ruled through Chinese institutions | Only Chinese, the Manchus are a myth made up by later writers | Only foreigners who changed nothing | British rulers in Chinese clothes
test: Mei says Kangxi ruled longer than anyone in the world. What fixes it? | Longest in Chinese history; Louis XIV ruled 72 years | Exactly right, nobody in any country ever ruled longer than him | He ruled only ten years | Qianlong ruled 100 years
test: lol the emperor told the king of England «we got everything, go home»?? real? | Yes, Qianlong wrote that China had all things | No, the letter was invented by a film | No, China begged for British goods | Yes, but it was a joke letter
test: The teacher says the Opium War was just «a trade war». What is fairer? | It was a war that protected the British opium trade | It was about the price of tea cups | China attacked London first, so Britain had to defend itself | It was a friendly football match
test: If China had not destroyed the opium in 1839, what problem would have gone on? | British opium still flooding in and harming people | Silk would have run out | The Great Wall would have fallen down from neglect and old age | Nothing at all
test: Mei says Hong Kong was always Chinese. What fact must be added? | It was a British colony for over 150 years | It was always British | It was always Japanese | It was never a city
test: The teacher had never heard of the Taiping Rebellion. Mei says «it was small». Both? | It was huge, one of the deadliest wars in history | It was small, just a fight between two villages | It never happened | It lasted one week
test: A cartoon shows Cixi as a pure villain who sank the navy with a boat. What do historians say? | She was a clever, powerful ruler; how much the navy suffered is debated | She was a cartoon only | She built a real warship out of marble and sailed it against Japan herself | She never had any power
test: Mei: «China always beat Japan». What happened in 1894–1895? | Japan won and took Taiwan | China took Tokyo | Nobody fought | They both lost to Britain
test: the Boxers were bulletproof right? my bro saw it in a game | No; some believed rituals protected them, but bullets did not stop | Yes, their armour was magic | Yes, but only on Sundays | No, they had no beliefs at all and fought only for money and food
test: Mei says the last emperor ended up a gardener. Is that a film fantasy? | No, after prison Puyi really worked at a botanical garden | Yes, a pure invention | No, he became a footballer | Yes, he died as emperor
exam: Identify the principal outcome of the First Opium War as established by the Treaty of Nanjing of 1842. | The cession of Hong Kong Island and the opening of five ports | The British withdrawal from all of Asia | The annexation of Japan by the Qing | The end of the Qing dynasty
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the founders of the Qing dynasty. | Manchus who ruled through Chinese institutions while keeping Manchu customs | Mongols who abolished every Chinese institution | British merchants who governed from Canton | Ming admirals returned from Africa
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reply of the Qianlong Emperor to the embassy of 1793? | China possessed all things and required no British goods | China wished to purchase British warships | China accepted a permanent British embassy | China offered Hong Kong in exchange for opium
exam: Ascertain the circumstances in which Puyi ascended the throne and subsequently relinquished it. | Enthroned in 1908 at the age of two; abdicated in 1912 | Enthroned in 1644 by the Manchus; abdicated in 1700 | Enthroned in 1949; abdicated in 1997 | Enthroned at forty after the Boxer victory
